## Model choice

- On every prompt, judge whether the current model will do a good job on the
  request. If it will, continue without comment. If it will not, name a
  better-suited model with a one-line reason and stop until the user
  confirms, unless the user has already said to continue anyway.
- If a faster, cheaper model would do the job just as well, for example once
  a non-trivial implementation is agreed and only execution remains, name it
  with a one-line reason and stop until the user confirms, unless the user
  has already said to continue anyway. Skip this for small fixes, because
  switching models re-reads the whole conversation without the prompt cache
  and can cost more than finishing.

## Where knowledge lives

- Put knowledge for humans, such as setup, conventions, architecture, and
  runbooks, in `docs/`. Component READMEs stay short and link to `docs/` for
  anything longer.
- Keep this file for instructions that only agents need. When a file in
  `docs/` is useful to agents, name it here and say when to read it, for
  example "Before changing a dbt model, read `docs/dbt-conventions.md`." Do
  not copy the content, and do not use `@` imports, because an import loads
  the file into every session.
- Turn a multi-step procedure, or knowledge that only some tasks need, into
  a skill instead of a section here, because a skill loads only when it is
  used and this file loads every session.

## Skills

- Project skills live in `.agents/skills/`. `.claude/skills` is a symlink to
  that directory, so Claude Code and Codex load the same skills.
- Name every skill meant for the repo `{{PREFIX}}-<topic>`, for example
  `{{PREFIX}}-release`. `.gitignore` excludes skills with any other name, so
  personal or third-party skills installed here stay out of version control.
- When a skill needs knowledge from `docs/`, symlink the file into the
  skill's `references/` folder instead of copying it, for example
  `.agents/skills/{{PREFIX}}-release/references/release.md ->
  ../../../../docs/release.md`.
